Meditation has been shown in clinical studies to lower cortisol levels, which directly correlates with reduced stress in the body.
Research indicates that regular meditation can decrease heart rate and blood pressure, both of which are physiological markers of stress.
The practice of meditation activates the parasympathetic nervous system, promoting relaxation and reducing the body's stress response.
Studies have found that individuals who meditate regularly report lower perceived stress levels, aligning with physiological changes.
The consistency of findings across multiple studies supports the claim that meditation influences the body's stress response mechanisms.